<section class="page-head">
  <p class="kicker">Limb IX · i — Magic &amp; the Wizard's Craft</p>
  <h1>The Occult</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">A word that means <em>hidden</em>, and almost nothing else. Not a religion, not a
  single tradition, not a secret handed down intact from Egypt. A long, documented, frequently reinvented
  European habit of treating the world as legible — of assuming that what is concealed can be read by
  someone who learns the grammar. This page gives the history with its real dates, the practices with their
  real mechanisms, and our stance stated plainly at the end.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p><em>Occultus</em> is the past participle of <em>occulere</em>, to cover over. In Latin astronomy an
    occultation is simply one body passing in front of another — the Moon occults a star. The word carried no
    glamour at all until the nineteenth century, when Éliphas Lévi and then the Theosophists made
    <em>l'occultisme</em> the name of a movement. ◆ Established. Everything older than about 1850 that we now
    file under "the occult" was called something else at the time: natural magic, the art, physic, astrology,
    philosophy.</p>

    <p>That matters more than it sounds. The category is modern even where its contents are ancient, and the
    modern category was assembled by people with motives — to unify, to sell, to claim descent, to resist a
    mechanical universe that seemed to have no room left for meaning. When an order tells you its lineage runs
    unbroken to the temple at Memphis, the honest response is not mockery. It is to ask for the manuscript,
    and to notice who benefits from the claim.</p>

    <p>Our working definition, used throughout this page: <strong>the occult is the body of practices and
    doctrines that treat the hidden structure of the world as something a trained person can read and act
    upon.</strong> Correspondence, symbol, ritual, name, number, hour. It overlaps with religion but is not
    it; it overlaps with early science and for two centuries <em>was</em> it; and it survives today in forms
    that range from careful contemplative work to outright fraud. We keep all three columns open —
    established, contested, ours — and never let the third be dressed as the first.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦ ✦ ✦</div>

