<section class="wrap">
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h3>What the word has meant</h3>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><strong>Hidden (classical).</strong> A property of things, not of people. Astronomical
        occultation; "occult qualities" in scholastic physics meant causes you could not see — magnetism was
        an occult quality until it wasn't. <span class="sci">◆</span></li>
        <li><strong>Natural magic (1450–1700).</strong> The legitimate study of hidden natural powers.
        Della Porta's <em>Magia Naturalis</em> contains optics, horticulture and cosmetics alongside
        sympathies. <span class="hist">◆</span></li>
        <li><strong>Occultism (1850–).</strong> A self-conscious movement and marketplace: Lévi, Blavatsky,
        the Golden Dawn, the Theosophical Society. <span class="hist">◆</span></li>
        <li><strong>The occult (popular, 1920–).</strong> A publishing and then a cinema genre, which is
        where most people meet the word and why it carries a smell of sulphur it did not earn.
        <span class="hist">◆</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h3>What it has never meant</h3>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><strong>A single continuous tradition.</strong> There is no unbroken chain. There are
        rediscoveries, translations, revivals and inventions, each datable.
        <span class="sci">◆</span></li>
        <li><strong>Devil worship.</strong> A charge laid by inquisitors and later by tabloids. The
        Satanic-ritual-abuse panic of the 1980s produced thousands of accusations and no corroborated case.
        <span class="sci">◆ Tested, negative</span></li>
        <li><strong>A rejection of reason.</strong> Newton wrote more on alchemy and prophecy than on
        optics. Kepler cast horoscopes for a living. The split is later than the work.
        <span class="hist">◆</span></li>
        <li><strong>Something only the credulous do.</strong> The membership of the Golden Dawn included a
        Nobel laureate poet, a professional chemist and a coroner.
        <span class="hist">◆</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦ ✦ ✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">A HISTORY, WITH DATES</p>
    <ul class="timeline">
      <li><span class="when">c. 1800 BCE</span> Babylonian celestial omen tablets — the <em>Enūma Anu
      Enlil</em> series. Not natal astrology: omens for the king and the state. The sky is already being
      read as a text.</li>
      <li><span class="when">c. 400 BCE</span> The zodiac of twelve equal signs appears in Babylon. Horoscopic
      astrology — a chart for an individual birth — follows in Hellenistic Egypt about two centuries
      later.</li>
      <li><span class="when">c. 100–300 CE</span> The Greek <em>Hermetica</em> are written in Roman Egypt:
      philosophical dialogues attributed to Hermes Trismegistus, blending Platonism, Stoicism and Egyptian
      temple religion. The Emerald Tablet, source of "as above, so below", is later still — Arabic, by the
      ninth century.</li>
      <li><span class="when">c. 800–1000</span> The great period of Arabic alchemy and magic: Jābir ibn
      Ḥayyān, al-Rāzī's laboratory procedure, the <em>Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm</em> — later Latinised as the
      <em>Picatrix</em>. Nearly everything medieval Europe later called its own came through this
      transmission.</li>
      <li><span class="when">1144</span> Robert of Chester finishes the first Latin translation of an Arabic
      alchemical text in Spain. The twelfth-century translation movement hands Europe Aristotle, Ptolemy,
      algebra and alchemy in the same cartload.</li>
      <li><span class="when">1463</span> Ficino, ordered by Cosimo de' Medici, drops his Plato to translate a
      newly arrived Greek <em>Hermetica</em> first. Europe reads it as a revelation older than Moses. It is
      the single most consequential mistranslation of an age in Western esotericism.</li>
      <li><span class="when">1486</span> Pico della Mirandola's <em>900 Theses</em> introduce Christian
      Kabbalah. The same year, the <em>Malleus Maleficarum</em> is printed. Learned magic rises and the witch
      trials begin together — and the people burned were overwhelmingly not practitioners of learned magic
      but poor women with reputations.</li>
      <li><span class="when">1533</span> Agrippa's <em>De occulta philosophia</em>, in three books: natural,
      celestial, ceremonial. Still the architecture of nearly every magical system written since.</li>
      <li><span class="when">1582–89</span> John Dee and Edward Kelley record the Enochian sessions. Dee was
      Elizabeth I's astrologer, a serious mathematician and the man who wrote the preface to the first English
      Euclid. The same mind did both.</li>
      <li><span class="when">1614</span> Isaac Casaubon dates the <em>Hermetica</em> by their Greek to the
      Christian era. The claim of Mosaic antiquity collapses. The tradition carries on regardless — which is
      itself the most interesting fact in this timeline.</li>
      <li><span class="when">1614–16</span> The Rosicrucian manifestos circulate in Kassel. An invisible
      college of adepts is announced; no such college is ever found. The fraternity is then founded by people
      responding to the rumour. A myth creating its own referent — and a pattern you will see again.</li>
      <li><span class="when">1666–93</span> Newton's alchemical notebooks: over a million words, kept private,
      suppressed by his heirs, sold at Sotheby's in 1936 and bought largely by Keynes, who called him "the
      last of the magicians".</li>
      <li><span class="when">1784</span> The French royal commission on Mesmer — Franklin, Lavoisier, Guillotin
      — invents the blinded control to test animal magnetism, finds no fluid, and finds that the effects are
      real and produced by imagination. The first modern test of an occult claim, and the birth of placebo
      research.</li>
      <li><span class="when">1856</span> Éliphas Lévi's <em>Dogme et rituel de la haute magie</em>. The word
      <em>occultisme</em> enters general use; the tarot is wired into the Kabbalah, on no historical warrant
      whatsoever, and the wiring holds to this day.</li>
      <li><span class="when">1875–88</span> The Theosophical Society (Blavatsky, Olcott) and then the
      Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn (Westcott, Mathers, Woodman). Theosophy imports a garbled India;
      the Golden Dawn synthesises Agrippa, Kabbalah, Egypt and Freemasonry into the grade system almost every
      later order copies. Yeats, Maud Gonne and Crowley pass through it.</li>
      <li><span class="when">1882</span> The Society for Psychical Research is founded by Cambridge
      philosophers to test the claims honestly. It exposes a long run of fraudulent mediums, including
      Blavatsky's Mahatma letters in 1885, and it has never stopped being the most useful thing the period
      produced.</li>
      <li><span class="when">1904–47</span> Crowley's Thelema; Gurdjieff's Fourth Way; Steiner's
      Anthroposophy. The movement fragments into schools with named founders, which is what a living
      tradition looks like when it stops pretending to be ancient.</li>
      <li><span class="when">1912–45</span> <em>Ariosophy</em> and the <em>völkisch</em> occult in
      Austria and Germany. List and Lanz von Liebenfels invent a racial prehistory; Himmler builds the
      SS Ahnenerbe on it. Handled in full below, and handled as what it was.</li>
      <li><span class="when">1947–54</span> Gerald Gardner publishes; modern Wicca begins. Its claim to be a
      surviving pagan religion was disproved by Hutton and others in the 1990s. It is roughly as old as the
      transistor, and that is no argument against anyone's practice — only against the origin story.</li>
      <li><span class="when">1960s–80s</span> Chaos magic (Spare, then Carroll and Sherwin) strips the
      lineage out altogether: belief as a tool, results as the only test. The most intellectually honest
      current in the field, and the one most willing to say it does not know.</li>
      <li><span class="when">1980–95</span> The Satanic panic. Recovered-memory therapy, daycare trials,
      no corroborating evidence ever produced, and real lives destroyed. The occult's worst modern moment
      belongs to its accusers, not its practitioners.</li>
      <li><span class="when">2000–now</span> Esotericism becomes a university discipline — Amsterdam,
      Exeter, the EASR — while online practice explodes without any gatekeeping at all. The two have barely
      met. This page is a small attempt at introducing them.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

