<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker" id="black-tribunal">LIMB VIII — THE BLACK TRIBUNAL</p>
    <p>The order holds an eighth limb — the <strong>Black Tribunal</strong>, Limb VIII — precisely so this
    material has somewhere to be examined rather than quietly dropped. Edward Gregory has, through meditation
    and ritual recall, identified a past-life character who stood inside the Nazi regime. He does not present
    this as evidence of anything to anyone else, and neither do we. He presents it as a working requirement:
    an <strong>enforced integration</strong> with the feelings, choices and paths of previous beings, those
    that impacted positively and those that impacted negatively alike, with nothing disowned and nothing
    exiled to a convenient other.</p>
    <p>That is the opposite of the occult-fascist move. Ariosophy worked by projection — the pure ancestor,
    the contaminating outsider, the stolen inheritance to be reclaimed by force. Integration refuses the
    projection and takes the material back inside, where it costs the practitioner something. If a symbolic
    system cannot survive being turned on the person using it, it is not a discipline. It is a flag.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦ ✦ ✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h3>How the order uses this material</h3>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><strong>Date everything.</strong> Before adopting a practice, find out when it was written. If
        the answer is 1888 or 1954, use it anyway — but say 1888.</li>
        <li><strong>Separate the mechanism from the metaphysics.</strong> A candle, an hour and a stated
        intention do measurable things to attention and memory. That is enough to justify the practice without
        a single supernatural claim.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep the three columns.</strong> Established, contested, ours. A practice can sit in the
        third column for a lifetime and be none the worse, provided it is never moved left by wanting.</li>
        <li><strong>Test what can be tested.</strong> If a claim makes a difference in the world, it can be
        blinded. If it cannot be blinded even in principle, be honest that it is a value, not a finding.</li>
        <li><strong>Refuse hierarchy by secrecy.</strong> Grades and oaths are a governance technology. Where
        secrecy exists to protect a teaching, examine it; where it exists to protect a teacher, leave.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h3>Where the harm actually is</h3>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><strong>Medical delay.</strong> The one lethal failure mode. Nothing on this site is medical
        advice; no practice here replaces a doctor.</li>
        <li><strong>Financial extraction.</strong> Paid initiations, escalating readings, courses sold on
        fear. The oldest fraud in the field and still the commonest.</li>
        <li><strong>Coercive groups.</strong> Isolation from outsiders, sexual access by rank, unpayable
        debts of gratitude. The structure matters far more than the doctrine.</li>
        <li><strong>Racialised lineage claims.</strong> Blood, soil, purity, ancestral right. Any system that
        sorts people by descent has told you what it is.</li>
        <li><strong>Private certainty.</strong> The quiet one. A practice that only ever confirms what the
        practitioner already believed has stopped being enquiry.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">GLOSSARY</p>
    <dl class="glossary">
      <dt>Hermeticism</dt><dd>The philosophy of the Greek <em>Hermetica</em> (c. 100–300 CE): a living,
      intelligible cosmos, humanity poised between matter and mind, knowledge as ascent. Not Egyptian in the
      pharaonic sense, despite its own claim.</dd>
      <dt>Esotericism</dt><dd>The academic term, preferred to "occult" because it carries no verdict.
      Faivre's four marks: correspondences, living nature, imagination as an organ of knowledge, and
      transmutation of the practitioner.</dd>
      <dt>Theurgy</dt><dd>From Iamblichus: ritual aimed at the operator's own ascent, not at commanding
      spirits. The respectable half of late antique magic.</dd>
      <dt>Goetia</dt><dd>The other half — the evocation of named spirits, from the Greek for howling. The
      <em>Lesser Key of Solomon</em> is a seventeenth-century compilation of much older lists.</dd>
      <dt>Kabbalah / Qabalah</dt><dd>Jewish mysticism from twelfth-century Provence and Spain; the second
      spelling usually signals the Christianised, then Golden Dawn, adaptation, which is a different thing
      with the same furniture.</dd>
      <dt>Egregore</dt><dd>A group mind generated and sustained by collective attention. Nineteenth-century
      French in origin, from the Greek <em>egrēgoros</em>, "watcher". Read conservatively it names something
      perfectly real: groups do develop characters their members did not individually choose. The order is
      named for the honest version of it.</dd>
      <dt>Ariosophy</dt><dd>The Austro-German racial-occult current of List and Lanz von Liebenfels,
      c. 1900–1930. Pseudo-runology in service of a racial prehistory. Named here so it is never mistaken for
      tradition.</dd>
      <dt>Grimoire</dt><dd>A book of operations — names, hours, seals, conditions. Most famous examples are
      early modern, not medieval, and most circulated in cheap print, not in locked libraries.</dd>
      <dt>Sigil</dt><dd>A desire compressed into a glyph and then deliberately forgotten. Spare's method,
      c. 1913, and the clearest case in the field of a technique that works on attention and makes no claim
      about the world.</dd>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">READING — THE HONEST SHELF</p>
    <ul class="biblio">
      <li><strong>Frances Yates</strong>, <em>Giordano Bruno and the Hermetic Tradition</em> (1964). The book
      that made the field respectable. Overstates its thesis; still indispensable.</li>
      <li><strong>Wouter Hanegraaff</strong>, <em>Western Esotericism: A Guide for the Perplexed</em> (2013).
      The current scholarly baseline. Start here.</li>
      <li><strong>Ronald Hutton</strong>, <em>The Triumph of the Moon</em> (1999). Dates modern pagan
      witchcraft precisely and sympathetically, which is harder than either debunking or believing.</li>
      <li><strong>Nicholas Goodrick-Clarke</strong>, <em>The Occult Roots of Nazism</em> (1985). The careful
      account — including the limits of the claim. Read it before anyone's conspiracy version.</li>
      <li><strong>Lawrence Principe</strong>, <em>The Secrets of Alchemy</em> (2013). A working chemist
      reconstructs the recipes. Deflates the mystique and raises the respect.</li>
      <li><strong>Owen Davies</strong>, <em>Grimoires: A History of Magic Books</em> (2009). Follows the actual
      objects and who bought them.</li>
      <li><strong>Shawn Carlson</strong>, "A double-blind test of astrology", <em>Nature</em> 318 (1985).
      Short, fair, designed with astrologers' agreement.</li>
      <li><strong>Peter J. Carroll</strong>, <em>Liber Null &amp; Psychonaut</em> (1987). For the practitioner's
      side, by someone who refuses to claim ancestry.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

